icc_shell> man write_verilog
2.  Synopsys Commands                                        Command Reference
                                 write_verilog

NAME
       write_verilog
              Writes a hierarchical Verilog file for the current design.

SYNTAX
       status write_verilog
               [-pg]
               [-split_bus]
               [-empty_module]
               [-no_physical_only_cells]
               [-no_pg_pin_only_cells]
               [-no_corner_pad_cells]
               [-no_pad_filler_cells]
               [-no_core_filler_cells]
               [-no_flip_chip_bump_cells]
               [-no_cover_cells]
               [-no_chip_cells]
               [-no_io_pad_cells]
               [-no_tap_cells]
               [-no_tsv_cells]
               [-no_unconnected_cells]
               [-unconnected_ports]
               [-keep_backslash_before_hiersep]
               [-diode_ports]
               [-wire_declaration]
               [-output_net_name_for_tie]
               [-macro_definition]
               [-force_output_references reference_names]
               [-force_no_output_references reference_names]
               [-supply_statement all | none]
               [-top_only]
               [-verbose]
               verilog_file_name

   Data Types
       reference_names       list
       verilog_file_name     string

ARGUMENTS
       -pg    Writes  power  and  ground nets and ports for all cell instances
              and module instances. By default, the power and ground ports are
              ignored.

       -split_bus
              Writes  bus ports as individual bits. The port names are treated
              as scalar port names and are escaped.

              For example, if you have the following module definition:

                   module top (out, in);
                     output [3:0] out;
                     input in;
                     ...
                   endmodule

              It is written as:

                   module top(\out[3] , \out[2] , \out[1] , \out[0] , in);
                     output \out[3] ;
                     output \out[2] ;
                     output \out[1] ;
                     input \out[0] ;
                     output in;
                     ...
                   endmodule

       -empty_module
              Writes empty module definitions for leaf-level  cells,  such  as
              standard cells and macro cells. These module definitions contain
              port definitions but no internal module instances.  By  default,
              no module definitions are written for leaf-level cells.

       -no_physical_only_cells
              Prevents  the  writing  of  physical-only  cell  instances. This
              option is equivalent to using both the -no_pg_pin_only_cells and
              -no_core_filler_cells options.

       -no_pg_pin_only_cells
              Prevents  the  writing of cell instances that have only power or
              ground pins and that do not fall into the other five subtypes of
              physical-only cells.

       -no_corner_pad_cells
              Prevents the writing of corner pad cell instances.

       -no_pad_filler_cells
              Prevents the writing of pad filler cell instances.

       -no_core_filler_cells
              Prevents the writing of core filler cell instances.

       -no_flip_chip_bump_cells
              Prevents the writing of flip-chip bump cell instances.

       -no_cover_cells
              Prevents the writing of cover cell instances.

       -no_chip_cells
              Prevents the writing of chip cell instances.

       -no_io_pad_cells
              Prevents the writing of I/O pad cell instances.

       -no_tap_cells
              Prevents the writing of tap cell instances.

       -no_tsv_cells
              Prevents   the   writing   of  through-silicon  via  (TSV)  cell
              instances.

       -no_unconnected_cells
              Prevents the writing of unconnected cell  instances.  These  are
              instances  with  no  ports connected except for power and ground
              ports.

       -unconnected_ports
              Writes unconnected ports on module instances,  macro  instances,
              and  standard  cell instances. All unconnected ports are written
              with a "SYNOPSYS_UNCONNECTED_%d" net name. The "%d"  portion  of
              the  name  represents  the  unconnected  net  number,  which  is
              increased incrementally each time a new  net  is  generated.  By
              default, the unconnected ports are ignored.

       -keep_backslash_before_hiersep
              Keeps  the  backslash  character preceding a hierarchy separator
              for all identified instances. By default, the backslash  charac-
              ter is removed.

       -diode_ports
              Writes  diode  ports for cell instances. By default, diode ports
              are ignored.

       -wire_declaration
              Writes wire declarations in the hierarchical Verilog description
              for  scalar  wires. For bus wires, a declaration with bus limits
              is always written, irrespective  of  this  option  setting.   By
              default, the command writes wire declarations only for buses.

              For example, if you use -wire_declaration, wires are written as:

                   wire n246;
                   wire [1:0] n245;
                   BUF U12 (.A(n245[0]), .Y(n246));

              If you do not use -wire_declaration, the wires are written as:

                   wire [1:0] n245;
                   BUF U12 (.A(n245[0]), .Y(n246));

       -output_net_name_for_tie
              Writes an indirect tie net as its net name. By default, indirect
              tie  nets  are written out as 1'b1 for logic 1 or 1'b0 for logic
              0.  This option setting only affects the writing of indirect tie
              nets.   A direct tie net has the name SNPS_LOGIC1 or SNPS_LOGIC0
              and is always written out as 1'b1 for logic 1 or 1'b0 for  logic
              0.

       -macro_definition
              Writes  module  definitions  for soft macros.  A soft macro is a
              user-partitioned macro that is generated during design planning.
              Most  big  designs  tend  to be divided into several partitioned
              macros.  When you use this  option,  the  Verilog  writer  looks
              inside  the  soft macro and writes out the internal details. The
              full contents of the cell are also written.

              By default, a soft macro is treated as a leaf macro and its def-
              inition is not written.

       -force_output_references reference_names
              Specifies  the  reference cell names, separated by space charac-
              ters, for which all cell instances must be written. This  option
              overrides the following options:
                   -no_physical_only_cells
                   -no_pg_pin_only_cells
                   -no_corner_pad_cells
                   -no_pad_filler_cells
                   -no_core_filler_cells
                   -no_tap_cells
                   -no_tsv_cells
                   -no_cover_cells
                   -no_chip_cells
                   -no_flip_chip_bump_cells
                   -no_io_pad_cells
                   -no_unconnected_cells
              The maximum allowed string size is 1023 characters.

              For  example,  consider  a  cell  "top"  with four types of core
              filler cells: FILL1, FILL2, FILL4, and FILL8.  To  generate  the
              Verilog description with only FILL4 and FILL8 instances, use the
              -no_core_filler_cells option  and  specify  -force_output_refer-
              ences {FILL4 FILL8}.

       -force_no_output_references reference_names
              Specifies  the  reference cell names, separated by space charac-
              ters, for which all cell instances must not be written.

       -supply_statement all | none
              By default, the write_verilog command skips generation of supply
              statements  for  PG nets connected with a power switch.  In that
              case, change_names is required before using write_verilog.

              With the -supply_statement all, option, write_verilog  generates
              supply  statements  for  all  PG nets, irrespective of the power
              switch output port connections.

              With the -supply_statement none option, write_verilog skips gen-
              eration  of supply statements for all PG nets in the output Ver-
              ilog file.

       -top_only
              Writes only the top modules for the current  design.   Only  the
              top module is generated, when applicable.

       -verbose
              Generates more information in the report.

       verilog_file_name
              Specifies the name of the file to which the hierarchical Verilog
              description is written. This is a required argument.

DESCRIPTION
       This command generates a  Verilog  netlist  for  the  current  Milkyway
       design.   By specifying different options, you can control the behavior
       of this command.

       Before running the write_verilog command, you  should  always  run  the
       change_names  -rule  verilog  -hierarchy command.  This command ensures
       correct Verilog  naming  conventions  and  also  identifies  the  power
       switches present in the design.

       If  you  run the change_names command before running write_verilog, the
       power switches are not written to the Verilog netlist. Otherwise, power
       switches are written by using supply1 statements.

   Multicorner-Multimode Support
       This command has no dependency on scenario-specific information.

EXAMPLES
       The  following  example  shows the most common use of the write_verilog
       command.

         prompt> write_verilog top_hvo.v
         Generating description for top level cell.
         Processing module sub
         Processing module top
         Elapsed = 0:00:00, CPU = 0:00:00
         Write verilog completed successfully.

SEE ALSO
       change_names(2)
       read_verilog(2)

                          Version N-2017.09-SP4
            Copyright (c) 2018 Synopsys, Inc. All rights reserved.
